21:29:32 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It is completely understandable why you would have that impression. When we rely on the news to understand the world, our perception is shaped by what gets the most coverage. Because attacks by groups like ISIS or Al-Qaeda have been heavily covered by Western media over the last two decades, it can absolutely feel like they represent the whole religion, or that they are the only ones committing these acts. However, when we look at the actual data and broader global context, the reality is quite different. Here is a breakdown of why this truly is a tiny minority, and why the news can sometimes give us a skewed picture: **1. The Math: A Fraction of a Percent** There are roughly 1.9 billion Muslims in the world today, making up about 24% of the global population. If even 1% of Muslims were violent extremists, that would mean 19 million terrorists globally. In reality, intelligence estimates put the numbers of active members in these extremist groups in the tens of thousands. That means that upwards of **99.99% of Muslims have absolutely nothing to do with terrorism.** They are ordinary people going to work, raising families, and living peaceful lives. **2. The Primary Victims are Other Muslims** A fact that rarely makes the front page in Western news is that the vast majority of victims of Islamic extremist violence are Muslims themselves. Groups like ISIS and Boko Haram have killed far more Muslims in the Middle East and Africa than they have people of other faiths. Extremists often target other Muslims who do not agree with their radical, violent political ideologies. **3. It Isn't "Always" Islamic Extremism** While Islamic extremism receives a massive amount of international media attention, it is not the only source of terrorism. For example, in the United States, the FBI and the Department of Homeland Security have repeatedly stated in recent years that domestic violent extremism—particularly racially or ethnically motivated extremism and anti-government extremism—poses the most lethal and consistent terrorist threat inside the country. Terrorism is a tactic used by many different political, nationalist, and ideological groups worldwide; it is not exclusive to any one religion or demographic. **4. How the News Works** The news is designed to report on things that are dangerous, unusual, and frightening. A news broadcast will never run a headline that says, "1.9 Billion Muslims Went to Work and Had a Normal Day Today," because peace and normalcy aren't "news." This creates a psychological effect called the *availability heuristic*: because we can easily recall vivid news stories about Islamic extremists, our brains trick us into thinking it is a much more common occurrence than it mathematically is. **5. Religion as a Political Tool** When extremists commit violence "in the name of religion," they are usually using religious language to justify political, territorial, or financial goals. Throughout history, extremists of *all* backgrounds—including Christian, Hindu, and Buddhist extremists—have cherry-picked religious texts to justify violence. In a world religions course, you will learn how to distinguish between a religion's actual historical theology and the way modern political groups twist that theology for their own gain. It is good that you are asking these questions. Critical thinking means looking past the headlines and asking what the data actually says. Taking a world religions course will give you the exact historical and sociological tools you need to understand these complex global issues.
